    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm trying to get a handle on some inheritance tax issues for my family. We have assets spread across the UK and a few other countries, and it's getting pretty complicated. Can you explain how tax residency usually works when determining which country has the right to tax an inherited estate?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tax residency is a crucial factor because it determines which countries have the primary right to tax your estate. Generally, the country where the deceased was considered a tax resident at the time of death will have significant taxing rights. Many countries, however, have bilateral tax treaties that override domestic rules to prevent double taxation of the same assets. Therefore, the interplay between the deceased's residency and the location of the assets is key to understanding your obligations.",
      "source": "target",
