    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One of the main things we're worried about is the complexity of the different asset types. We have some commercial properties in the UK, but also some liquid assets like stocks and cash held in overseas accounts. How do tax authorities typically distinguish between these when deciding where the tax should be paid?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tax authorities often apply principles based on the source of the income or assets, rather than just the physical location. For immovable assets like UK commercial property, the situs (location) of the asset usually dictates the taxing jurisdiction. Conversely, liquid assets like stocks and cash are frequently taxed based on the worldwide residence of the beneficiaries or the deceased, according to treaty rules. The specific tax laws of each relevant jurisdiction will dictate which classification and taxing principle applies to each asset type.",
      "source": "target",
